Medicare Part B premiums are deducted directly from Social Security checks for most beneficiaries. When the Part B premium increases, it absorbs a portion of your cost-of-living adjustment (COLA).

The Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) announced the final 2027 Medicare Part B premium on November 17, 2026, in a CMS fact sheet. The standard monthly premium rises from $202.90 to $209.40, an increase of $6.50.

For example, consider a retiree with a $2,000 monthly benefit who receives a $72.00 COLA raise:

- Gross COLA increase: plus $72.00 a month
- Part B increase from $202.90 to $209.40: minus $6.50 a month
- Net monthly gain: $65.50

Under the hold-harmless provision, the $6.50 increase cannot reduce your net monthly check below what you received the previous year. If your COLA raise is less than the $6.50 increase, Medicare adjusts your premium so your take-home benefit does not decline.